# Philosophy audit ledger - round 3

Verdict: NOT SATISFIED (P1 major; P2 minor). All 45 catalog quotations match the specified standard translations; no material misquotation or work/translator misattribution found.

Reviewed at commit 9985432. Freshly fetched primary editions, all 45 served quotations compared on both required devices, all 22 sessions and all 111 authored rounds read. See `coverage.json` for each source, session and round. This is a content audit, not the overall design/flow verdict.

## Round 2 recheck

- R2-1: fixed. Conditional diary conclusion, three fresh test days, reduced score and firstTry=false on failed predictions, visible-shape limitation. Live confirmation belongs to late-games review.
- R2-4: fixed. b2-1 now explains the number word's role in the activity; no plural-suffix instruction. Early-games live recheck confirms it.
- R2-5: fixed. Signposts uses PI198/202; Going on includes PI217. Keyed session map at PupilGame.tsx:19; late-games completed both live.

## P1 - major - A circular check is treated as sufficient to establish a hinge

Where: http://localhost:3417/play/hinges, h1-0; src/games/hinges/rounds.ts:18,38,45,52; src/games/choice/activities.tsx:434. Live evidence: `phone-eyes-circular.png`, `desktop-eyes-circular.png`, `hinges-live.json`.

Choose Read the sign -> My eyes are working -> Read the small print. The game calls this check circular, marks a hinge found, and says it stands fast. Its opening instruction says the target could only be checked by relying on itself. The same rule is applied to knowing the meanings of particular words. It teaches the wrong sufficient condition: a deliberately circular menu makes an ordinary corrigible claim a hinge. Ordinary eyesight tests or checking a particular word can hold other resources fixed; the game never distinguishes that from doubting all such resources together. It does not literally assert infallibility, and the general source card correctly says hinge status depends on situation; those facts do not repair this task's demonstrated inference.

Primary comparison: OC125 concerns a doubt about one's hands that would also undermine the offered visual confirmation. OC349 expressly considers ordinary eyesight testing; OC345 distinguishes what cannot simultaneously be questioned during a particular inquiry. See [Paul & Anscombe 1969, printed 18e-19e](https://www.circuitdebater.org/circuitdebater/archive/w/file/view/On%20Certainty.pdf/507953768/On%20Certainty.pdf#page=21), [printed 44e-45e](https://www.circuitdebater.org/circuitdebater/archive/w/file/view/On%20Certainty.pdf/507953768/On%20Certainty.pdf#page=47), rendered and read in `oc-page-21.png`, `oc-page-22.png`, `oc-page-47.png`, `oc-page-48.png`.

Concrete fix: let an ordinary eyesight/word-meaning check succeed using an independently supplied target or another person while other checking practices remain fixed. Then explicitly pose simultaneous radical doubt about the whole practice, showing why that removes its means of checking. Reward recognizing the change of context, not discovering a self-loop in an artificially restricted graph.

## P2 - minor - A valid written sign-language link is rejected

Where: http://localhost:3417/play/family, f3-3, What is a language?; src/games/family/rounds.ts:75,88,192. Live evidence from early-games: `../early-games/edges/phone-signlanguage-written.png`, `desktop-signlanguage-written.png`, `sign-probe.json`.

English -> sign language -> can be written down is rejected as false on both devices. The card data categorically excludes writing; the generic card does not specify an unwritten sign language. This is an authored factual error, not a claim in PI18. Other valid links allow progress, so it is local rather than a blocked session. [SignWriting's own description](https://www.signwriting.org/about/what/what02.html) documents a writing system for signed languages. The primary page was retrieved with Python after browser fetch timeouts and is retained as `signwriting-primary.html`.

Concrete fix: allow written sign language and accept this link. If discussing how common writing is, use a qualified feature with an explanation; do not equate uncommon standard orthography with inability to write. Recheck the chain and no-common-feature constraint after correcting the data.

